<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">René-Robert Cavelier, Sieur de La Salle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1643">1643</a>–<a href="/years/1687">1687</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as René-Robert Cavelier de La Salle, La Salle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">René-Robert Cavelier, Sieur de La Salle, was a French explorer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. After exploring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> region, in 1682 he travelled down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> and claimed its basin for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, naming it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>. His 1684 expedition to found a colony at the river's mouth landed instead on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, and he was murdered by his own men in 1687.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rene-robert-cavelier-sieur-de-la-salle.jpg" alt="René-Robert Cavelier, Sieur de La Salle" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles André Waltner (1846-1925) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cavelier_de_la_salle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Robert Cavelier was born on 22 November 1643 into a comfortably well-off family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>. He studied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> and took initial vows in 1660, which required him to give up his father's legacy, so he was nearly destitute when he sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> in the spring of 1666. On 27 March 1667 he was released from the Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, citing moral weaknesses. He then received a seigneurie at the western end of the Island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>, which became known as Lachine. The title Sieur de La Salle came from a family estate near Rouen, and he took it with his purchase of Lachine from the Sulpician order around 1667.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His first expedition, which left Lachine on 6 July 1669, was meant to find a river the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> had described, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, as a western passage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The factual record of it ends in obscurity, and La Salle himself never claimed to have discovered the Ohio. In 1673 he was left in command of the fort at Cataraqui, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-frontenac">Fort Frontenac</a>, built to control the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a>, and he secured the fort's grant and letters of nobility during a voyage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1674–75.</p><p>In December 1678 he and Henri de Tonti were the first Europeans documented to have seen Niagara Falls. The ship Le Griffon, launched on 7 August 1679, vanished on its return from Green Bay with a load of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>.</p><p>In January 1680 La Salle and Tonti built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ussher-fort">Fort Crèvecoeur</a> on the Illinois River, and soldiers there mutinied and destroyed it while he was away. He reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> in February 1682, and on 9 April 1682 he claimed the river's basin for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, naming it La Louisiane in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 24 July 1684 La Salle left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> with four ships and 300 colonists to found a colony at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>. Pirates took one ship, another sank, and the flagship La Belle ran aground, stranding the colony on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> coast on Garcitas Creek. After several attempts on foot to find the river, he was killed on 19 March 1687 by Pierre Duhaut in an ambush, after some of his men had mutinied.</p><p>The colony lasted until 1688, when Karankawa-speaking people killed the 20 remaining adults and took five children captive. Only 15 of the original 180 colonists at the fort are known to have survived. La Salle's alliances with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a> and other peoples cemented the French policy of containing both Iroquois influence and Anglo-American colonisation. La Belle was found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matagorda-bay">Matagorda Bay</a> in 1995.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “René-Robert Cavelier, Sieur de La Salle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>French explorers charted much of the interior: Louis Jolliet and Jacques Marquette descended the Mississippi in 1673, <b>René-Robert Cavelier de La Salle</b> reached its mouth in 1682 and claimed Louisiana for Louis XIV, and the La Vérendrye family pushed west towards the prairies in the 1730s and 1740s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Reaching the mouth of the Mississippi, <b>René-Robert Cavelier de La Salle</b> claimed its whole drainage basin for Louis XIV and named it Louisiana.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-9">9 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1682 <b>René-Robert Cavelier de La Salle</b> travelled down the Mississippi and claimed its basin for Louis XIV, naming it Louisiana.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-louisiana">French Louisiana</a></li><li><blockquote>Alarmed by <b>La Salle's</b> French colony on Matagorda Bay, Spain sent Alonso de León to east Texas, where the mission of San Francisco de los Tejas was founded among the Hasinai Confederacy in 1690.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-texas">Spanish Texas</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Huntsville, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.44,1.1&amp;year=1671" aria-label="Rouen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of René-Robert Cavelier, Sieur de La Salle"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5061 5897h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5061 5897h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.44,1.1&amp;year=1671">What was here in 1671? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
